==English==
{{was wotd|2015|October|5}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|hinderland||dated}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|de|Hinterland}}, from {{compound|de|hinter|Land|t1=behind|t2=land|nocat=1}}, cognate to {{cog|en|hinder||back, rear}} + {{m|en|land}}. First used in English in 1888 by {{w|George Chisholm}} in his work ''Handbook of Commercial Geography'' originally as {{m|en|hinderland}}, but the current spelling (following German) became more popular.<ref>{{cite-web|title=hinterland|titleurl=https://www.britannica.com/science/hinterland|work={{w|Encyclopædia Britannica}}|accessdate=20 September 2019}}</ref> The term is characteristic of a [[thalassocracy|thalassocratic]] analysis of space (from the point of view of a nation with maritime supremacy, such as 19th-century Britain).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhɪntə(ɹ)ˌlænd/|/-lənd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-hinterland.ogg|a=AU}}

